UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 21, Lesson 1: Printmaking basics', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Printmaking makes copies of an image. A printing plate holds the design, such as a piece of foam, a potato half or a found object like a leaf. You add a thin layer of paint or ink to the plate, press it onto paper and lift it, and the image appears reversed, like a mirror. You can repeat the same print many times to make a pattern. Use only a thin layer of paint, press evenly and keep your workspace covered. An adult should cut any firm materials such as potatoes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou want to print the letter ''S'' on a card. Explain what you need to do to the plate so the print looks the right way round, and how much paint to use.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur the lines.'' Prints are mirror images of the plate, and thin paint gives clear lines.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake four prints using a leaf, a sponge or a potato (cut by an adult). Test thin and thick paint and write which worked better. Arrange your best prints in a repeating pattern and label the pattern. Cover your table and wash hands afterwards.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Printmaking makes copies of an image. A printing plate holds the design, such as a piece of foam, a potato half or a found object like a leaf. You add a thin layer of paint or ink to the plate, press it onto paper and lift it, and the image appears reversed, like a mirror. You can repeat the same print many times to make a pattern. Use only a thin layer of paint, press evenly and keep your workspace covered. An adult should cut any firm materials such as potatoes.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make four prints using a leaf, a sponge or a potato (cut by an adult). Test thin and thick paint and write which worked better. Arrange your best prints in a repeating pattern and label the pattern. Cover your table and  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur the lines.'' Prints are mirror images of… Reasoning: I used a print by attending to a printing plate rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best. Correction: Strong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a print.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a print on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a print, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur… This matches the lesson skill for a print.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur…","Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best."],"question":"You want to print the letter ''S'' on a card. Explain what you need to do to the plate so the print looks the right way round, and how much paint to use. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Strong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur…","Careful use of a printing plate with clear evidence.","Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best.","Printmaking makes copies of an image. A printing plate holds the design, such as a piece of foam, a potato half or a found object like a leaf. You add a thin…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a print?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a printing plate, not by copying the model.","options":["Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a printing plate used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising a print. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a print?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a print.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a printing plate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: Nina draws a fish on a foam tile with a pencil, pressing grooves in the foam. She rolls blue paint on the tile, lays paper on…","Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Printmaking makes copies of an image. A printing plate holds the design, such as a piece of foam, a potato… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best.","Make four prints using a leaf, a sponge or a potato (cut by an adult). Test thin and thick paint and write which worked better. Arrange your best…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a print?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a print on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur the lines.'' Prints are mirror images of… Reasoning: I used a print by attending to a printing plate rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a print, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best. Correction: Strong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a print.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w21-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a print.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use a print with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a printing plate and explain why it matters.","I can practise ink or paint independently on a new prompt.","I can notice repeating needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Printmaking makes copies of an image. A printing plate holds the design, such as a piece of foam, a potato half or a found object like a leaf. You add a thin layer of paint or ink to the plate, press it onto paper and lift it, and the image appears reversed, like a mirror. You…","options":["Printmaking makes copies of an image. A printing plate holds the design, such as a piece of foam, a potato half or a found object like a leaf. You add a thin layer of paint or ink to the plate, press it onto paper and lift it, and the image appears reversed, like a mirror. You can repeat the same print many times to make a pattern. Use only a thin layer of paint, press evenly and keep your workspace covered. An adult should cut any firm materials such as potatoes.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a print?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Nina draws a fish on a foam tile with a pencil, pressing grooves in the foam. She rolls blue paint on the tile, lays paper on top, rubs gently and lifts it. The fish print appears with the grooves showing as white lines, and the fish faces the opposite direction to the plate. She repeats the print four times in a row to make a shoal.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYou want to print the letter ''S'' on a card. Explain what you need to do to the plate so the print looks the right way round, and how much paint to use.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''I need to carve or draw a reversed S on the plate, because the print flips it. I will use a thin layer of paint, because too much will blur the lines.'' Prints are mirror images of the plate, and thin paint gives clear lines.","explain":"Printmaking makes copies of an image. A printing plate holds the design, such as a piece of foam, a potato half or a found object like a leaf. You add a thin layer of paint or ink to the plate, press it onto paper and lift it, and the image appears reversed, like a mirror. You can repeat the same print many times to make a pattern. Use only a thin layer of paint, press evenly and keep your workspace covered. An adult should cut any firm materials such as potatoes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that thick paint gives a stronger print. Too much paint squashes out, fills the details and makes a smudged print, so a thin, even layer works best. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Plate and printed image.","caption":"A print is a mirror.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Plate</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">design pressed in foam</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Print</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">image appears reversed</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Nina draws a fish on a foam tile with a pencil, pressing grooves in the foam. She rolls blue paint on the tile, Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Nina draws a fish on a foam tile with a pencil, pressing grooves in the foam. She rolls blue paint on the tile, Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a print?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Nina draws a fish on a foam tile with a pencil, pressing grooves in the foam. She rolls blue paint on the tile, lays paper on top, rubs gently and lifts it. The fish print appears with the grooves showing as white lines, and the fish faces the opposite direction to the plate. She repeats the print four times in a row to make a shoal. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Thin and thick paint.","caption":"Thin layers print clearly.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org